  <!-- 1. The two totals -->
  <section class="formula-block" id="the-two-totals">
    <h2>The two totals</h2>
    <p>
      Every other calculator shows one number. This shows two, and the gap is the reason the project exists. The
      <strong>lender total</strong> is what underwriting counts; the <strong>true total</strong> adds the one thing
      it doesn't, the maintenance you'll actually spend.
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>lender</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo>
        <mi>P&amp;I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>MI</mi><mo>+</mo>
        <mi>HOA</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>M</mi>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>true</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo>
        <msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>lender</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>R</mi>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <dt>P&amp;I</dt><dd>principal and interest (below)</dd>
      <dt>T, I</dt><dd>property tax, homeowners insurance</dd>
      <dt>MI</dt><dd>mortgage insurance (PMI, FHA MIP, or none)</dd>
      <dt>HOA, M</dt><dd>HOA dues, Mello-Roos / special assessments</dd>
      <dt>R</dt><dd>maintenance reserve, 1% of value a year by default</dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="source">
      A lender never counts maintenance, so it must never inflate a debt-to-income ratio.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/mortgage.ts">mortgage.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 2. Monthly payment -->
  <section class="formula-block" id="the-mortgage-payment">
    <h2>The mortgage payment</h2>
    <p>The standard amortized-loan formula: the fixed monthly payment that retires a loan of <math><mi>P</mi></math> over <math><mi>n</mi></math> months at monthly rate <math><mi>r</mi></math>.</p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo>
        <mi>P</mi><mo>·</mo>
        <mfrac>
          <mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo>)</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow>
          <mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo>)</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow>
        </mfrac>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <dt>P</dt><dd>the loan amount, including any financed FHA upfront premium or VA funding fee</dd>
      <dt>r</dt><dd>the annual rate divided by 12</dd>
      <dt>n</dt><dd>the term in months, so 360 for a 30-year loan</dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="source">
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/amortization.ts">amortization.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 3. Balance and LTV over time -->
  <section class="formula-block" id="the-balance-and-when-mortgage-insurance-">
    <h2>The balance, and when mortgage insurance ends</h2>
    <p>
      After <math><mi>k</mi></math> payments the remaining balance is the loan grown at the rate, less the payments
      made, also grown. Loan-to-value is that balance over the original purchase price.
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo>
        <mi>P</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo>)</mo><mi>k</mi></msup>
        <mo>−</mo>
        <mi>M</mi><mo>·</mo>
        <mfrac>
          <mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo>)</mo><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow>
          <mi>r</mi>
        </mfrac>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <p>
      Conventional PMI ends at two automatic points under the Homeowners Protection Act, whichever comes first: the
      month LTV reaches 78% <em>on the original schedule</em> (appreciation does not count), or the midpoint of the
      term. FHA is different: under 10% down, its premium runs for the <strong>life of the loan</strong>, no equity
      exit at all.
    </p>
    <p class="source">
      Homeowners Protection Act of 1998; HUD Mortgagee Letter 2023-05 for FHA.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/mortgage.ts">mortgage.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- FHA MIP -->
  <section class="formula-block" id="fha-mortgage-insurance">
    <h2>FHA mortgage insurance</h2>
    <p>
      FHA charges an <strong>upfront</strong> premium of 1.75% of the loan, financed into the balance, plus an
      <strong>annual</strong> premium paid monthly. The annual rate depends on the term and loan-to-value; for a
      standard 30-year loan it is 0.55% above 95% LTV and 0.50% below.
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <msub><mi>MIP</mi><mtext>mo</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo>
        <mfrac><mrow><mi>rate</mi><mo>·</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mn>12</mn></mfrac>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <p>
      The part that hurts is the <strong>duration</strong>, and it turns on the down payment, not equity: under 10%
      down, the premium runs for the <em>life of the loan</em>, no equity level removes it, and the only exit is
      refinancing off FHA. 10% or more down buys an exit at 11 years.
    </p>
    <p class="source">
      HUD Mortgagee Letter 2023-05.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/data/programs.ts">programs.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Conventional PMI -->
  <section class="formula-block" id="conventional-pmi">
    <h2>Conventional PMI</h2>
    <p>
      Below 20% down, a conventional loan carries private mortgage insurance, priced off the lender's rate card by
      loan-to-value and credit score. The range is wider than most quotes admit: 0.19% for a 760+ borrower just
      under 20% down, up to 1.85% at 97% LTV with a sub-640 score, 8.5x the rate for the same house.
    </p>
    <p>
      It terminates at 78% LTV on the original schedule (appreciation does not count), or at the midpoint of the
      term, whichever comes first. These are estimates, flagged as such: insurers don't publish their cards.
    </p>
    <p class="source">
      Homeowners Protection Act of 1998; rate bands cross-checked against the Urban Institute.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/data/programs.ts">programs.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- VA funding fee -->
  <section class="formula-block" id="the-va-funding-fee">
    <h2>The VA funding fee</h2>
    <p>
      VA loans carry no mortgage insurance but a one-time funding fee, a percentage of the loan that scales with
      the down payment and whether you have used a VA loan before. A service-connected disability rating at any
      level waives it entirely.
    </p>
    <table class="method-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Down payment</th><th>First use</th><th>Subsequent use</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>0%</td><td>2.15%</td><td>3.30%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5–10%</td><td>1.50%</td><td>1.50%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>10%+</td><td>1.25%</td><td>1.25%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="source">
      va.gov. The fee is measured on the base loan before it is financed in, so rolling it in does not push you
      into a worse mortgage-insurance band.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/data/programs.ts">programs.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Loan limits -->
  <section class="formula-block" id="loan-limits-conforming-and-fha-are-diffe">
    <h2>Loan limits: conforming and FHA are different</h2>
    <p>
      Both the conforming limit (FHFA) and the FHA limit are set at 115% of the county's area median sale price,
      capped at 150% of the national baseline. The difference is the floor:
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <mi>limit</mi><mo>=</mo>
        <mi>clamp</mi><mo>(</mo>
        <mn>1.15</mn><mo>·</mo><msub><mi>median</mi><mtext>county</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo>
        <mspace width="0.3em"/><mtext>floor</mtext><mo>,</mo>
        <mspace width="0.3em"/><mn>1.5</mn><mo>·</mo><mtext>baseline</mtext>
        <mo>)</mo>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <p>
      FHFA floors at the full baseline; FHA floors at 65% of it. Below the ceiling the two diverge hard:
      <strong>Stanislaus is $832,750 conforming and $545,100 FHA.</strong> Over its limit an FHA loan is not a
      jumbo, it is simply not an FHA loan, and the engine carries both limits on every scenario so it can never
      grab the wrong one.
    </p>
    <p class="source">
      FHFA county file; HUD CHUMS master file.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/data/ca-fha-limits.ts">ca-fha-limits.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 4. Property tax -->
  <section class="formula-block" id="property-tax-under-proposition-13">
    <h2>Property tax, under Proposition 13</h2>
    <p>
      Buying resets the assessed value to your purchase price. The tax is that value, less the homeowners'
      exemption if you file for it, times the county rate.
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo>
        <mo>(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>)</mo>
        <mo>·</mo><mi>t</mi>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <dt>V</dt><dd>the purchase price (Prop 13 reassesses to it)</dd>
      <dt>E</dt><dd>the $7,000 homeowners' exemption, if claimed</dd>
      <dt>t</dt><dd>the county rate: the 1% statutory base plus voter-approved bonds</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Mello-Roos is modeled as its own line, never folded into <math><mi>t</mi></math>, because doing so would
      double-count and hide the single most invisible cost in California new construction.
    </p>
    <p class="source">
      California Constitution Article XIII A. County rates are hand-verified where we have them, and flagged as a
      statewide fallback where we do not.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/data/ca-property.ts">ca-property.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Cash to close -->
  <section class="formula-block" id="cash-to-close">
    <h2>Cash to close</h2>
    <p>What you actually need on hand at signing: the down payment, closing costs, and the escrow account seeded up front.</p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <mi>Cash</mi><mo>=</mo>
        <mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo>
        <mn>0.025</mn><mo>·</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo>
        <mo>(</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>I</mi><mo>)</mo>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <dt>D</dt><dd>the down payment; plus any upfront fee you pay in cash rather than finance</dd>
      <dt>0.025 · V</dt><dd>closing costs, estimated at 2.5% of price: lender fees, appraisal, title, escrow</dd>
      <dt>6T + 3I</dt><dd>six months of property tax and three of insurance, collected to open the impound account</dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="source">
      Closing costs and impound months are estimates, flagged as such.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/mortgage.ts">mortgage.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- DTI -->
  <section class="formula-block" id="debt-to-income-and-the-income-a-house-ne">
    <h2>Debt-to-income, and the income a house needs</h2>
    <p>
      Underwriting counts two ratios: the front end is housing against income, the back end adds every other
      monthly debt. The back end is the one that binds.
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <msub><mi>DTI</mi><mtext>back</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo>
        <mfrac>
          <mrow><mi>Housing</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Debts</mi></mrow>
          <msub><mi>Y</mi><mtext>mo</mtext></msub>
        </mfrac>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <p>Inverting it gives the income a given house needs, against the program's ceiling:</p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <msub><mi>Y</mi><mtext>req</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo>
        <mfrac>
          <mrow><mn>12</mn><mo>(</mo><mi>Housing</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Debts</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow>
          <mi>ceiling</mi>
        </mfrac>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <p>
      Ceilings run about 45% conventional, 43–57% FHA. VA is the exception: it has no hard ceiling, and its 41%
      figure is a guideline, so for a VA loan the binding test is residual income, below, and the income figure is
      solved from that instead.
    </p>
    <p class="source">
      Fannie Mae Selling Guide B3-6-02; HUD Handbook 4000.1.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/mortgage.ts">mortgage.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 5. VA residual income -->
  <section class="formula-block" id="va-residual-income">
    <h2>VA residual income</h2>
    <p>
      VA does not underwrite on debt-to-income; it underwrites on what is left over each month after everything.
      That residual has to clear a regional minimum, and California is in the West region, which has the highest
      minimums in the country.
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <mi>Residual</mi><mo>=</mo>
        <msub><mi>Y</mi><mtext>mo</mtext></msub>
        <mo>−</mo><mi>Tax</mi>
        <mo>−</mo><mi>Housing</mi>
        <mo>−</mo><mi>Debts</mi>
        <mo>−</mo><mn>0.14</mn><mo>·</mo><mi>ft²</mi>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <dt>Y<sub>mo</sub></dt><dd>gross monthly income across all earners</dd>
      <dt>Tax</dt><dd>estimated income-tax withholding</dd>
      <dt>0.14 · ft²</dt><dd>VA's fixed utility allowance, 14¢ per square foot per month</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      This is why a VA borrower can be approved at a debt-to-income ratio that would sink a conventional loan, and
      why the 41% figure you may have heard is a guideline, not a cap.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/mortgage.ts">mortgage.ts</a>,
      VA Lender's Handbook M26-7 ch. 4.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 6. Rent versus buy -->
  <section class="formula-block" id="rent-versus-buy-two-net-worth-paths">
    <h2>Rent versus buy: two net-worth paths</h2>
    <p>
      The honest version of "rent is throwing money away". Each year, the <strong>buyer's</strong> net worth is
      what the house would fetch after selling costs, less the loan balance:
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>buy</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo>
        <mi>V</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>)</mo>
        <mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <p>
      The <strong>renter</strong> invests the money the buyer sank into the house, and every month adds or
      withdraws the difference in the two paths' cash costs, compounding at the investment return:
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>rent</mtext></msub><mo>←</mo>
        <msub><mi>W</mi><mtext>rent</mtext></msub><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>inv</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo>
        <mo>+</mo><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>own</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>rent</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <p>
      Owning does not always <em>stay</em> ahead. When the investment return you give up beats the appreciation
      you buy, the renter's invested capital eventually out-compounds the house: owning leads in a middle window,
      then renting reclaims the lead. So the panel reports whether owning is ahead <em>at your hold year</em>, not
      merely whether it ever crossed.
    </p>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <dt>V, B</dt><dd>the home value and loan balance that year</dd>
      <dt>s</dt><dd>selling costs, 6% by default</dd>
      <dt>c<sub>own</sub>, c<sub>rent</sub></dt><dd>the monthly cost of owning (net of the tax deduction) and of renting</dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="source">
      Every cost that enters this comes from one <code>bridgeScenario()</code> call, so the itemised comparison and
      every price sweep cannot drift apart.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/rent-vs-buy.ts">rent-vs-buy.ts</a>,
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/scenario-bridge.ts">scenario-bridge.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 7. The mortgage interest deduction -->
  <section class="formula-block" id="the-mortgage-interest-deduction">
    <h2>The mortgage interest deduction</h2>
    <p>
      Only the interest above the standard deduction is worth anything, and only on the first $750,000 of debt.
      The relief in a year is:
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <mi>Relief</mi><mo>=</mo>
        <mi>τ</mi><mo>·</mo>
        <mi>max</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo>
        <mi>min</mi><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mtext>ded</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo>
        <msub><mi>i</mi><mtext>ded</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>O</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <dt>τ</dt><dd>combined marginal tax rate</dd>
      <dt>i<sub>ded</sub></dt><dd>deductible interest, capped at the share of the loan under $750,000</dd>
      <dt>O</dt><dd>other itemized deductions</dd>
      <dt>S</dt><dd>the standard deduction for your filing status</dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="source">
      Internal Revenue Code §163(h); the 2026 standard deduction and SALT cap.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/data/federal-tax.ts">federal-tax.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 8. Affordability -->
  <section class="formula-block" id="what-can-you-actually-afford">
    <h2>What can you actually afford?</h2>
    <p>
      Run backwards, this is a search, not a formula. Price feeds back into tax, insurance, the PMI band, and the
      jumbo and FHA limits, so there is no closed form. The engine binary-searches for the highest price that still
      clears every gate: the debt-to-income ceiling, VA residual income, and the FHA county limit, a hard stop that
      is not the conforming limit.
    </p>
    <p class="source">
      An answer above the conforming limit is quoted at the conforming rate, so it is an upper bound, and the page
      says so on the number.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/affordability.ts">affordability.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 9. Buying power -->
  <section class="formula-block" id="buying-power-the-payment-formula-inverte">
    <h2>Buying power: the payment formula, inverted</h2>
    <p>
      To ask "what could a median income buy", invert the payment. Given a monthly budget of 30% of income at that
      year's rate, the affordable price is:
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <mi>Price</mi><mo>=</mo>
        <mfrac>
          <mn>1</mn>
          <mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow>
        </mfrac>
        <mo>·</mo>
        <mi>M</mi><mo>·</mo>
        <mfrac>
          <mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo>)</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow>
          <mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo>)</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow>
        </mfrac>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <dt>M</dt><dd>the monthly budget: 30% of gross income, divided by 12</dd>
      <dt>d</dt><dd>the assumed down payment, 20%</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Holding the <em>effort</em> constant at 30% and letting the price float is what isolates buying power from
      everything else that moved.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/buying-power.ts">buying-power.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 10. The deposit race -->
  <section class="formula-block" id="saving-the-deposit-with-rent-rising">
    <h2>Saving the deposit, with rent rising</h2>
    <p>
      A renter's saving capacity is not fixed: rising rent eats into it. If non-rent spending tracks wages, next
      year's monthly saving is:
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>next</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo>
        <mi>s</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>)</mo>
        <mo>+</mo>
        <mi>rent</mi><mo>·</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mtext>rent</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <dt>g</dt><dd>nominal wage growth, 3.51% from the California income series</dd>
      <dt>g<sub>rent</sub></dt><dd>rent growth, which you set</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      When rent grows faster than wages the second term goes negative and the saving rate falls, so a renter whose
      rent outruns wages closes the gap more slowly. The house price, meanwhile, compounds at its own appreciation
      rate, so the deposit is a genuine race.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/rent-vs-buy.ts">rent-vs-buy.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- The price index in dollars -->
  <section class="formula-block" id="the-price-history-in-dollars">
    <h2>The price history, in dollars</h2>
    <p>
      The FHFA repeat-sales index tracks what the <em>same</em> houses resold for, but it has no units. To read it
      in dollars, anchor it to Zillow's current typical single-family value for the county and scale every past
      reading against the latest:
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <msub><mi>price</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo>
        <mi>anchor</mi><mo>·</mo>
        <mfrac>
          <msub><mi>index</mi><mi>t</mi></msub>
          <msub><mi>index</mi><mtext>latest</mtext></msub>
        </mfrac>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <p>
      So the dollar figures describe one representative house through time, not the median listing of each year.
      Every ratio the panels report divides the anchor out, so only the dollar axis depends on it. Metro counties
      get a quarterly series; the 21 rural counties get their own annual one.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/history.ts">history.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Drawdowns -->
  <section class="formula-block" id="crash-detection-drawdowns">
    <h2>Crash detection: drawdowns</h2>
    <p>
      A "crash" is a peak-to-trough decline of at least 10% on that index. Running-peak logic collapses
      overlapping dips into the single real drawdown rather than double-counting them: track the highest reading
      seen, and when a new high arrives, close off any decline of
      <math><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>trough</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>peak</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>peak</mi><mo>≤</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>%</mi></mrow></math>
      that came before it.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is what makes the crash presets each county's own: "the shallowest of the 3 declines on record" is
      derived, never written down, because 17 counties have one decline and 16 have three.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/history.ts">history.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Should you wait -->
  <section class="formula-block" id="should-you-wait-for-a-crash">
    <h2>Should you wait for a crash?</h2>
    <p>
      Waiting has a cost the "just wait" crowd never counts: the rent you pay and the principal you don't pay down
      while you wait. It also has a benefit nobody counts: a permanently lower Prop 13 basis. The break-even is how
      long the monthly saving takes to repay the rent burned getting there.
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <msub><mi>months</mi><mtext>break-even</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo>
        <mfrac>
          <msub><mi>rent</mi><mtext>waiting</mtext></msub>
          <msub><mi>saving</mi><mtext>monthly</mtext></msub>
        </mfrac>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <p>
      The surprising result, pinned by a test: because the lower assessed value follows you for as long as you
      own, Prop 13 raises the crash size at which waiting finally beats buying now. Price is not payment, and a
      decline that arrives with rate relief is worth far less than the same decline at today's rate.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/history.ts">history.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Cohort -->
  <section class="formula-block" id="why-an-earlier-buyer-pays-less-decompose">
    <h2>Why an earlier buyer pays less, decomposed</h2>
    <p>
      The panel prices the same house at an earlier date via the repeat-sales index, then splits the monthly gap
      into its three causes, which sum exactly to the total:
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <mi>Δ</mi><mo>=</mo>
        <munder><munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>PI</mi><mtext>you</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>PI</mi><mtext>their rate</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⏟</mo></munder><mtext>rate</mtext></munder>
        <mo>+</mo>
        <munder><munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>PI</mi><mtext>their rate</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>PI</mi><mtext>them</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⏟</mo></munder><mtext>price</mtext></munder>
        <mo>+</mo>
        <munder><munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>tax</mi><mtext>you</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>tax</mi><mtext>them</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Prop 13</mtext></munder>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <p>
      The decomposition is asserted to sum to the total in the tests, so the three bars can never quietly stop
      adding up. It also credits the earlier buyer with any refinance into the 2020–21 cheap-money window, if they
      already owned when it opened.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/cohort.ts">cohort.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Correlations -->
  <section class="formula-block" id="do-the-signals-actually-lead-the-market">
    <h2>Do the signals actually lead the market?</h2>
    <p>
      Each indicator is correlated against the next 24 months of price change with a plain Pearson coefficient. The
      honest part is the sample size: overlapping windows inflate it, so the effective count deflates the raw one
      by the window length.
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo>
        <mfrac>
          <mrow><mo>∑</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mover><mi>x</mi><mo>‾</mo></mover><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mover><mi>y</mi><mo>‾</mo></mover><mo>)</mo></mrow>
          <msqrt><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mover><mi>x</mi><mo>‾</mo></mover><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>∑</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mover><mi>y</mi><mo>‾</mo></mover><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt>
        </mfrac>
        <mo>,</mo>
        <mspace width="0.6em"/>
        <msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>eff</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo>
        <mfrac><mi>n</mi><mtext>window</mtext></mfrac>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <p>
      A handful of declines is not enough to fit anything to, and the effective <math><mi>n</mi></math> is printed
      precisely so nobody quotes the inflated one. The forecaster built on this data is shipped
      <em>failing its own pre-registered tests</em>, on purpose.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/signals.ts">signals.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Pay trap -->
  <section class="formula-block" id="the-pay-trap-where-the-arithmetic-still-">
    <h2>The pay trap: where the arithmetic still works</h2>
    <p>
      Measured against each county's <em>own</em> Census income, not the statewide figure, the correlation between
      what a county pays and how many years of that pay a house costs is
      <math><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>0.71</mn></mrow></math>; between pay and price outright it is
      <math><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>0.91</mn></mrow></math>. The better a county pays, the worse its
      housing multiple: the raise does not get you in, it gets bid away. Both are the same Pearson coefficient as
      above, computed at render time and pinned by tests.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/where-it-works.ts">where-it-works.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Rent cap -->
  <section class="formula-block" id="the-ab-1482-rent-cap">
    <h2>The AB 1482 rent cap</h2>
    <p>
      California caps annual rent increases at 5% plus regional inflation, never above 10%. The inflation figure is
      not one number for the state; it is the CPI for the region the property sits in.
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <mi>cap</mi><mo>=</mo>
        <mi>min</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>%</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>CPI</mi><mtext>region</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo>
        <mspace width="0.3em"/><mn>10</mn><mi>%</mi><mo>)</mo>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <p>
      It is a ceiling, not a forecast: most sitting tenants see far less, and the exemptions are broad. But it is
      the honest upper bound on how fast a landlord can raise a rent you are already in.
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/data/ca-rent-cap.ts">ca-rent-cap.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Inflation -->
  <section class="formula-block" id="old-dollars-in-today-s-money">
    <h2>Old dollars in today's money</h2>
    <p>
      Where a chart can be shown inflation-adjusted, past dollars are restated with the CPI. This changes the shape
      of the chart, never a ratio: both lines carry the same factor, so the headline findings do not move.
    </p>
    <math display="block">
      <mrow>
        <msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>today</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo>
        <msub><mi>V</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>·</mo>
        <mfrac>
          <msub><mi>CPI</mi><mtext>latest</mtext></msub>
          <msub><mi>CPI</mi><mi>t</mi></msub>
        </mfrac>
      </mrow>
    </math>
    <p class="source">
      <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}/blob/main/lib/buying-power.ts">buying-power.ts</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Everything else -->
  <section class="formula-block" id="the-rest-and-the-discipline-behind-it">
    <h2>The rest, and the discipline behind it</h2>
    <p>
      A few things are data, not formulas, under the same rule: the FAIR Plan wildfire figures (parsed from the
      insurer's own quarterly ZIP reports, refusing to write unless they reconcile to the printed totals), the
      county tax rates (real where we have them, a flagged statewide fallback where we don't), and the insurance
      premium (a statewide estimate, because no agency publishes a county average for the normal market). The
      supplemental tax bill is warned about but never modeled: that needs the seller's old assessed value, which we
      don't have, and a faked number is what this project exists to avoid.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every constant traces to a source with a real date and an honest label. The whole engine is a few thousand
      lines of TypeScript with no dependencies, and it is <a href="{{ site.repo.url }}">open source</a>, so you can
      read all of it and check any of it.
    </p>
    <p class="method-page__back"><a href="/">← Back to the calculator</a></p>
  </section>
</article>
